WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.067
at this stage of the course, you're
probably familiar with the relational

2
00:00:04.067 --> 00:00:08.345
database model, but to fully understand
how a relational database model works,

3
00:00:08.345 --> 00:00:12.066
you first need to understand how
tables within a database are related.

4
00:00:12.066 --> 00:00:16.906
Essentially, relationships are established
between tables with the use of keys.

5
00:00:16.906 --> 00:00:21.663
By the end of this video, you'll be able
to identify the main keys used in tables

6
00:00:21.663 --> 00:00:26.434
in a relational database and explain
the relationship between keys in a table.

7
00:00:26.434 --> 00:00:30.413
The relational database model
is based on two main concepts,

8
00:00:30.413 --> 00:00:35.858
entities which are defined as tables and
relations that connect to related tables.

9
00:00:35.858 --> 00:00:40.376
To realize how this model works, you need
to understand the different key attributes

10
00:00:40.376 --> 00:00:43.340
that exist in the relational
database to demonstrate.

11
00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:47.186
Let's use the example of a sports
competition that uses three tables to keep

12
00:00:47.186 --> 00:00:48.275
track of the league.

13
00:00:48.275 --> 00:00:52.373
The league table, the teams table and
the points table.

14
00:00:52.373 --> 00:00:55.081
Each table has relevant columns where each

15
00:00:55.081 --> 00:00:58.472
column represents an attribute
of the table entity.

16
00:00:58.472 --> 00:01:02.567
The league table keeps track of
each team's position in the league,

17
00:01:02.567 --> 00:01:05.045
their name and the state they represent.

18
00:01:05.045 --> 00:01:09.709
The team's table tracks the team name,
the team captain and the team coach, and

19
00:01:09.709 --> 00:01:13.277
the points table records
the team's position in the league,

20
00:01:13.277 --> 00:01:16.861
the team's name and
how many points the team has this season.

21
00:01:16.861 --> 00:01:19.922
Notice that the team's
table includes team name,

22
00:01:19.922 --> 00:01:22.348
which also belongs to the league table.

23
00:01:22.348 --> 00:01:26.761
These attributes could be of a simple
attribute type that can hold a single

24
00:01:26.761 --> 00:01:30.530
value, for example,
in a table of staff members in a college,

25
00:01:30.530 --> 00:01:33.892
each staff name attribute as
a single value in each row.

26
00:01:33.892 --> 00:01:37.842
Or they could also have a multi
value attribute that can have

27
00:01:37.842 --> 00:01:41.082
multiple values like
a list of subjects taught.

28
00:01:41.082 --> 00:01:46.418
However multi value attributes should be
avoided in relational database design.

29
00:01:46.418 --> 00:01:49.975
You'll learn more about this
concept later in the course.

30
00:01:49.975 --> 00:01:53.870
Let's use the example of the staff
table to explore some examples of

31
00:01:53.870 --> 00:01:54.906
attribute keys.

32
00:01:54.906 --> 00:01:56.768
Let's begin with the key attribute.

33
00:01:56.768 --> 00:02:01.880
This is a value used to uniquely identify
an individual record of data in a table.

34
00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:05.327
For example in the staff table
the key attribute is staff I.

35
00:02:05.327 --> 00:02:06.568
D.

36
00:02:06.568 --> 00:02:09.977
This attribute has unique value
in each row of the table so

37
00:02:09.977 --> 00:02:14.596
it's the perfect way to uniquely identify
each record of data in a relational

38
00:02:14.596 --> 00:02:18.521
database there are a range of
different types of key attributes.

39
00:02:18.521 --> 00:02:21.156
There's also the candidate key attributes.

40
00:02:21.156 --> 00:02:25.899
This is any attribute that contains
a unique value in each row of the table.

41
00:02:25.899 --> 00:02:28.432
In the case of the staff table,
both the staff I.

42
00:02:28.432 --> 00:02:29.103
D.
And

43
00:02:29.103 --> 00:02:32.128
contact numbers are examples
of candidate keys.

44
00:02:32.128 --> 00:02:34.804
Each has a new unique value in each role.

45
00:02:34.804 --> 00:02:38.058
The other columns can contain
repeated information so

46
00:02:38.058 --> 00:02:40.755
they're designated as non key attributes.

47
00:02:40.755 --> 00:02:43.820
A composite key is a key
that is composed of two or

48
00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:48.586
more attributes to form a unique value
in each new role in the staff table.

49
00:02:48.586 --> 00:02:52.023
An example of a composite key is
a combination of the staff name and

50
00:02:52.023 --> 00:02:55.152
staff title,
assuming that there isn't another instance

51
00:02:55.152 --> 00:02:57.730
of the same combination
elsewhere on the table.

52
00:02:57.730 --> 00:03:02.127
A composite key is usually considered
when a single attribute key can't be

53
00:03:02.127 --> 00:03:06.664
identified, A relational database must
also contain a primary key which you

54
00:03:06.664 --> 00:03:09.764
should already be familiar
with in the staff table.

55
00:03:09.764 --> 00:03:12.920
The staff idea is the primary key.

56
00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:17.050
An alternate key also known as
the secondary key is a candidate key that

57
00:03:17.050 --> 00:03:19.363
was not selected to be the primary key.

58
00:03:19.363 --> 00:03:21.065
Just like a primary key.

59
00:03:21.065 --> 00:03:24.053
It's a column that contains
a unique value in each field.

60
00:03:24.053 --> 00:03:29.180
For the staff table, the contact number
is a secondary key on each roll and

61
00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:31.507
finally there's a foreign key.

62
00:03:31.507 --> 00:03:35.729
The foreign key is an attribute on
the table that references a unique key in

63
00:03:35.729 --> 00:03:36.691
another table.

64
00:03:36.691 --> 00:03:40.291
Typically a foreign key reference is
the primary key of another table.

65
00:03:40.291 --> 00:03:41.769
For example, the staff I.

66
00:03:41.769 --> 00:03:44.242
D.
Might also be a foreign key in one or

67
00:03:44.242 --> 00:03:47.258
more tables within the college database.

68
00:03:47.258 --> 00:03:49.185
The relationship between primary and

69
00:03:49.185 --> 00:03:53.097
foreign keys will be discussed in more
detail At a later point in this course,

70
00:03:53.097 --> 00:03:57.090
you're now familiar with the different
types of keys in a relational database